s and target angle.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Dalton Schultz</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 CIN are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.47 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Mike Gesicki</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 HOU are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.34 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CIN throw it (64% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>HOU \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>HOU are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Woody Marks</strong> (12 carries, 45 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Nico Collins</strong> projects for <strong>6 targets and 55 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Dalton Schultz</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 29 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>CIN \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CIN stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>49%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Chase Brown</strong> (13 carries, 56 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong> projects for <strong>8 targets and 71 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 2-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CIN","col":"#FB4F14","players":[{"name":"Joe Burrow","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":247.98,"book":null,"over_th":234.89,"under_th":261.6,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.93,"book":null,"over_th":1.27,"under_th":1.66,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null}],"td":{"pct":9.5,"fair":"10/1","book":null,"value":null}},{"name":"Chase Brown","pos":"RB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Rush yards","proj":56.03,"book":null,"over_th":45.4,"under_th":55.48,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Carries","proj":12.92,"book":null,"over_th":11.57,"under_th":13.43,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Rec yards","proj":21.31,"book":null,"over_th":14.92,"under_th":19.68,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null},{"label":"Receptions","proj":3.15,"book":null,"over_th":2.83,"under_th":3.25,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"call":null,"ev":null,